      "value": "@{5b3926b2ba28ae3550c107e8|full_name}: Not exactly sure what you are getting at with the \"natural\" aspect. There is currently no natural way to control one's facial expressions given the technology currently available (in real life, you do not control your face with your hands). The concept you pose is certainly an interesting one, as it would create room for a very large set of animations to be accessed, but it imposes some technical and practical limitations. The most obvious being the high potential for confusion caused by natural hand movement. Imagine discovering you have been making a weird face for a while because you idly closed half your fingers without even thinking about it. Use of the touchstrip instead ensures that animations are triggered deliberately. Another design goal of using the touch strip is that by completely decoupling animations from the fingers, one can use whatever facial expression they want in conjuction with any hand gesture (I.E. hold three fingers up and smile or hold three fingers up and frown).",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
