  "details": "As the Knuckles Controllers are shaping up to feature a completely revamped control scheme, I feel it is worth exploring a revamped control scheme for VRChat on the Knuckles Controller.\n\n\nBASICS\nTo start, lets get the obvious controls out of the way:\nMovement->Left Thumbstick\nRotation->Right Thumbstick\nMenu->Left and Right B button\nJump->Right A button\n\n\nINTERACTION\nNow lets talk about separation of grabbing and selecting.\nInteraction with menus, buttons, chairs, and anything else you would \"click\" on should be handled with the triggers. To improve hand presence, the laser selector now projects from the index finger in game. Meanwhile, picking up objects will be handled exclusively with the force sensor on the grips. The Auto-Hold property should be ignored, as the Knuckles Controller should allow users to comfortably hold objects by lightly gripping them. Either the force sensor or the finger capacitive sensors can utilized to detect when an object is dropped. An intuitive combo would be to use the force sensor to detect initial pickup, but then use the capacitive sensors to require that all fingers let go of the controller to drop. While holding an object like a gun, the trigger can be used to fire it.\n\n\nHANDS AND ANIMATIONS\nNow we move on to big change: hand gestures and animation overrides. Fact is, the old hand gesture system is completely irrelevant to the Knuckles Controller. Therefore, it should go completely unused. Instead, hand gestures are controlled by the capacitive sensors in the face, trigger, and grips. This will give users individual control of each finger, allowing for a great variety of hand gestures to be made. In place of the old animation overrides for hand gestures, there will be a new set of animation overrides simply known as \"Custom\". These custom animation slots would work similarly to the current hand gesture overrides, except that hand gestures need  not apply, as these are specifically for facial animations, gun animations, etc. These animations can be triggered by \"clicking\" different areas of each touchstrip. My idea would be to break each touch strip into three sections, top, middle, and bottom for a total of six custom animations (three on each hand).\n\nEXTRA\nLacking a better dedicated use for the Left A button, I propose that it functions as a shortcut button. Simply point at one of the functions in the hand menu, then press the Left A button to assign it that function. With the hand menu closed, the Left A will perform that function. Possible use cases include one-button mute toggle, quick nameplate toggling, and quick access to emitting your favorite emoji.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI feel this control scheme would complement VRChat very well while adding some handy new capabilities. Feel free to give opinions on my proposal in the comments. I'll likely edit this proposal based on said comments.",
